      {
        "title": "OOMKilled",
        "source": "oomkilled.md",
        "section": "Symptoms",
        "text": "## Symptoms\n\n- Container `lastState.terminated.reason` is `OOMKilled`.\n- Restart count climbs; the pod may also show `CrashLoopBackOff` once the kubelet starts backing off.\n- Events may include `OOMKilling` from the kubelet. Logs often stop abruptly, sometimes in the middle of a request or a large allocation.\n- `kubectl describe pod` shows a memory `limit` that is close to, or below, what the process actually uses.",
        "score": 0.7627901261444132,
        "kind": "runbook"
      },
      {
        "title": "CrashLoopBackOff",
        "source": "crashloopbackoff.md",
        "section": "Common causes",
        "text": "## Common causes\n\n- The container command or entrypoint exits immediately (`exit 1`, a missing binary, or a typo in `args`).\n- The process crashes on startup because a required env var, ConfigMap key, or Secret is missing or malformed.\n- The application cannot reach a dependency (database, API, cache) and treats that as fatal.\n- A failed liveness probe kills a slow-starting process, which then crash-loops (see the probe-failures runbook).\n- The process is being OOM-killed; that often surfaces as `OOMKilled` rather than a generic crash, but both can appear together. Check the last terminated state.",
        "score": 0.7352751290027397,
        "kind": "runbook"
      },
      {
        "title": "CrashLoopBackOff",
        "source": "crashloopbackoff.md",
        "section": "Diagnosis",
        "text": "## Diagnosis\n\n1. Read the last termination reason and exit code:\n\n   ```bash\n   kubectl describe pod <pod> -n <ns>\n   kubectl get pod <pod> -n <ns> -o jsonpath='{range .status.containerStatuses[*]}{.name}{\" \"}{.state}{\" \"}{.lastState.terminated}{\"\\n\"}{end}'\n   ```\n\n2. Always fetch previous logs if `restartCount > 0`:\n\n   ```bash\n   kubectl logs <pod> -n <ns> --previous --tail=200\n   kubectl logs <pod> -n <ns> --tail=200\n   ```\n\n3. Confirm the command that actually ran (`command` / `args` in the pod spec) and whether the image entrypoint expects different arguments.\n4. If logs mention a missing file, env var, or connection refused, inspect the related ConfigMap, Secret, and Service.",
        "score": 0.7194686565329296,
        "kind": "runbook"
      },
      {
        "title": "OOMKilled",
        "source": "oomkilled.md",
        "section": "Diagnosis",
        "text": "## Diagnosis\n\n1. Confirm the termination reason and the current limits:\n\n   ```bash\n   kubectl describe pod <pod> -n <ns>\n   kubectl get pod <pod> -n <ns> -o jsonpath='{.spec.containers[*].resources}'\n   ```\n\n2. Compare usage if metrics are available:\n\n   ```bash\n   kubectl top pod <pod> -n <ns> --containers\n   ```\n\n3. Read previous logs for allocation errors, heap dumps, or a sudden stop.\n4. Check whether the node itself is under memory pressure (`kubectl describe node <node>` and look for evictions or `MemoryPressure`).",
        "score": 0.7072180034184401,
        "kind": "runbook"
